bind-keycloak-ids.py -- give existing X3 accounts their Keycloak ids
=====================================================================

What it is for
--------------
When X3 switches to its Keycloak login backend, every X3 account
("handle") should carry the id of its Keycloak user (the "kc_id" entry in
x3.db).  Accounts made before the switch have none.  This script fills them
in, offline, while X3 is down for the switch: for each account with no
kc_id and exactly one Keycloak user of the same name (compared under IRC
case folding, as X3 compares names), it adds

    "kc_id" "<22-character id>";

to that account.  It changes nothing else: accounts that already have a
kc_id are never touched, and every account it is unsure about is left as it
is and listed in the report.

This matters more than it may look: with Keycloak logins on, X3 never binds
an account by a login.  An account the script leaves without a kc_id cannot
be logged in to at all (AUTH tells the user to ask a network operator, SASL
and login-on-connect fail, a login a server announces leaves the user
logged out in X3) until an oper binds it with REBIND.  A Keycloak user of
the same name is not taken as proof that it is the same account.

It needs only Python 3 (standard library); nothing to install.

Rules
-----
- Stop X3 first.  X3 rewrites x3.db while it runs.
- Run it on a COPY of x3.db, and keep the original until the new build has
  run happily for a while.
- Run it in report mode first; read the report; then run it again with
  --out.
- The script never writes its input.  --out must name a new file (it
  refuses an existing file, the --db file and the --conf file); so must
  --json.  After
  writing, it reads the new file back and checks that it equals the input
  plus exactly the reported kc_id entries; on any other difference it
  deletes the new file and exits 1.  It also exits 1 if the input changed
  while it ran (X3 still running).  A failed write (a full disk, say), any
  other failure while writing or checking, or Ctrl-C also deletes the new
  file; if even that deletion fails, the message names the partial file
  to delete by hand.  A file that was already there is never touched.
- The new file gets the input's mode, and its owner and group where the
  script may set them (run as root, or as the input's owner).  Where it
  may not, it prints a WARNING naming the chown to run: X3 must be able to
  read and rewrite the file, and one it cannot open makes it start with no
  accounts at all.

Keycloak access
---------------
It uses the same Keycloak settings as X3: keycloak_url, keycloak_realm,
keycloak_client_id and keycloak_client_secret in x3.conf's
"services" / "nickserv" block.  Flags override them:

    --keycloak-url U --realm R --client-id C --client-secret-file F

The secret is never taken from the command line: put it in a file (mode
600) and pass --client-secret-file, or let it come from x3.conf.  It is
never printed.

The client's service account needs to view users (realm-management role
"view-users"; "manage-users", which X3's client has, includes it).  The
script also reads the realm's user profile for the username rules (see
below); on Keycloak 26.4.7 a service account holding only manage-users can
read it.  If it cannot, the script says so and uses its defaults.  The
script only reads from Keycloak.

Running it
----------
    tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf [--json report.json]
    tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf --out x3.db.bound

The accounts are read from the x3.db section x3.conf names for NickServ
(dbs / NickServ / mondo_section, normally "NickServ").  If x3.conf gives
NickServ a file of its own, pass that file as --db.

Each account takes one Keycloak request, except names holding any of
the characters [ ] \ ^ { } | ~.  IRC case folding makes [ and {, ] and },
\ and |, ^ and ~ the same letter, but Keycloak's own search does not, so
the script asks for every spelling: 2 requests for one such character, 4
for two, and so on.  A name with more than 6 of them (over 64 requests)
is not looked up; it is listed under errors.

Exit status: 0 finished and nothing needs a look; 2 finished but some
lists below are not empty; 1 a usage error, a file that does not parse
(the message gives the line), a Keycloak setting missing or refused, or a
failed verification.

The report
----------
Each list names the accounts in it; the summary at the end gives the
counts: handles total, bound now, already bound, bound with no Keycloak
user of that name, shared id, mismatched, replaced id, ambiguous, errors,
no Keycloak user (split by whether Keycloak would accept the name, and how
many have an OpServ level), outside the current name rule, and the rules
that were used.  --json writes the same report, with the bound accounts
and their ids.

bound now
    No kc_id, exactly one Keycloak user of that name: gets that user's id.
    Nothing to do.

already bound
    Already has a kc_id, and Keycloak's user of that name has the same id.
    Left as it is.

bound, no Keycloak user of that name
    Already has a kc_id, but Keycloak has no user of that name: the user
    was deleted (then nobody can log in to the account through Keycloak),
    or renamed (then its first login under the new name makes a second X3
    account, since X3 does not follow renames yet).  Left as it is; look
    the id up in Keycloak and decide.

shared id
    Two or more accounts hold the same kc_id (an oper's REBIND can do
    that).  A login as that Keycloak user only reaches the account of its
    name, and while several hold it X3 never deletes the Keycloak user
    when one of them is unregistered.  Decide which account the user
    belongs to and REBIND the others.

mismatched
    Already has a kc_id, but Keycloak's user of that name has another id.
    Left as it is.  Usually the Keycloak user was deleted and made again,
    or renamed; decide which user the account belongs to, by hand.

replaced id
    No kc_id, and the one Keycloak user of that name has an id listed in
    the account's kc_id_prev.  This handle was detached from that Keycloak
    user on purpose (an oper's REBIND * moves kc_id into kc_id_prev), and
    X3 refuses a replaced id as the account's id, so the script does not
    bind it.  Left as it is.  If the account should belong to that user
    again, an oper must REBIND it once the new build runs.

ambiguous
    More than one Keycloak user matches the name under IRC case folding
    (for example "foo[1]" and "foo{1}").  Left as it is; settle it by hand
    in Keycloak, then run the script again.

errors
    The lookup failed (Keycloak down, refused, a strange answer), the
    account's Keycloak user is already the kc_id of another account, the
    account appears twice in the file, its kc_id is not a plain string,
    its kc_id_prev is neither a string nor a list of strings, or its name
    has too many bracket characters to look up (see above).
    Left as it is.  After five failed lookups in a row the script stops
    asking and lists the remaining accounts here.  Fix the cause and run
    the script again (on the original copy, or on the new file: accounts
    bound by the first run then count as already bound).

no Keycloak user
    No Keycloak user of that name, so no kc_id is added.  Such an account
    keeps its channels, notes and the rest in X3, but nobody can log in
    to it through Keycloak until an oper binds it with REBIND (X3 never
    binds an account by a login in Keycloak mode).  Each entry says
    whether Keycloak would accept the name at all, and the account's
    OpServ level when x3.db has one; accounts with a level are listed
    first, since an oper locked out matters most.

    An account that exists in X3 and LDAP but not in Keycloak usually has
    a name Keycloak refuses; with X3's shipped valid_account_regex
    (^[-_a-z0-9A-Z]{2,15}$) every new name is one Keycloak accepts, so
    this list should be short.  For each entry, decide:
    - The user should keep the account: make (or find) the Keycloak user
      -- under the same name, if Keycloak accepts it -- and run the script
      again, so it binds the account before the switch; after the switch,
      an oper binds it instead: /msg AuthServ REBIND *account <the
      user's id from the Keycloak admin console>.
    - The account is abandoned: leave it.  It expires as X3's other
      accounts do (with keycloak_writeback on, expiry deletes no Keycloak
      user for it: it has none), or an oper OUNREGISTERs it.
    - The name is one Keycloak refuses: nobody can log in under it (X3
      checks that the Keycloak user's name is the name given).  Give the
      user a Keycloak user under an acceptable name; with
      keycloak_autocreate its first login makes a new X3 account, and an
      oper can MERGE the old account into that one.

outside the current name rule
    Accounts whose names the current valid_handle_regex /
    valid_account_regex (or, with neither, X3's built-in letters, digits,
    "-" and "_") would refuse today.  Only that pattern is checked, not
    X3's length limit or bad-word list.  Informational: such accounts keep
    working, but nobody could register the name now.  If X3's pattern
    uses something Python reads differently (POSIX classes such as
    [[:alnum:]], backslashes in brackets, ...), the script stops and names
    the pattern.

Keycloak's username rules
    Read from the realm's user profile (the username attribute's
    validators): the length limits and any pattern are applied as the
    realm sets them; Keycloak's prohibited characters only when the realm
    lists that validator (the script carries Keycloak 26.4's list: it
    refuses < > & " ' $ % ! # ? § , ; : * ~ / \ | ^ = [ ] { } ( ) ` ,
    whitespace and control characters).  Any other validator is named as
    "not checked by this script".  If the profile cannot be read, the
    script uses --name-min / --name-max (default 2 and 30) and the
    prohibited list, and says so.  These rules only label the
    "no Keycloak user" entries; they never decide a binding.

At the switch
-------------
Before the day:
- Keycloak: X3's own confidential client (direct access grants, the email
  and profile scopes, a service account with realm-management
  manage-users), and NO pairwise subject mapper on it: X3 takes the ID
  token's "sub" as the user's id.  keycloak_url should be https: X3 reads
  the id and the name from the token without checking its signature and
  trusts them because they came over that connection.
- Users with a second factor (OTP) cannot log in through the password
  grant, so not through AUTH, login-on-connect or SASL PLAIN via X3;
  tell them, or keep OTP off for accounts that log in through X3.
- keycloak_timeout below the LOC_TIMEOUT the legacy servers really run
  with (3 seconds by default; check their ircd.conf), or their users see
  a failed login before X3's answer arrives.
- The Keycloak host's name must resolve reliably from X3's host: while a
  DNS lookup stalls, logins fail at keycloak_timeout (and on a libcurl
  older than 7.87.0, which X3 warns about at start-up, the stalled lookup
  also holds X3 up); pin it in /etc/hosts if in doubt.
- X3 does not follow a rename made in Keycloak yet: avoid renames.  A
  renamed user's login under the new name is refused (X3 makes no second
  account for a Keycloak user another account is bound to) and opers are
  told; rename the user back in Keycloak.

The switch:
1. Stop X3.
2. Copy x3.db, keeping its owner and mode, and keep the original
   untouched: cp -p x3.db x3.db.copy (as root, or as the user X3 runs as).
3. Report: tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf
   Read it; deal with what needs dealing with (see the lists above: an
   account left without a kc_id cannot be logged in to until an oper
   REBINDs it); repeat.
4. Write: tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf --out x3.db.bound
   (not x3.db.new: X3 writes its own temporary file under that name).
   Watch for a WARNING about the owner.
5. Check x3.db.bound's owner, group and mode against the original x3.db
   (ls -l); fix them if they differ.  Put x3.db.bound in place as X3's
   x3.db (keep the original elsewhere).
6. In x3.conf: ldap_enable 0, keycloak_enable 1 and the keycloak_* keys,
   keycloak_writeback 0 for the first start (with it on, the first
   expiry pass deletes the Keycloak users of every account that expired
   meanwhile), keycloak_id_reset 0.
7. Start the new build.  In its log, look for "Keycloak logins on" -- and
   for "Unable to open database file" (x3.db unreadable: stop X3 at once,
   fix the owner or mode, start again; a running X3 that could not read
   its database starts with no accounts and would write that out).
8. Once logins work and the expiry pass has run, turn keycloak_writeback
   on if X3 should delete Keycloak users and write oper levels (rehash).

Going back (rollback):
1. Stop X3.
2. Put the original x3.db back (the one kept in step 2/5), with its owner
   and mode.
3. Restore the LDAP configuration: ldap_enable 1 and its keys,
   keycloak_enable 0.
4. Start the previous build (or this one: with keycloak_enable 0 it runs
   as before).  Accounts made in Keycloak mode since the switch exist only
   in the x3.db that was replaced; anything users changed meanwhile in X3
   is lost with it.
